<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  mini_ranka.urdf.xacro — the curated robot model: Onshape CAD geometry on the
  firmware's kinematic convention. This is the model the digital twin drives.

  DERIVED FROM ../../full_assembly/urdf/full_assembly.urdf (raw Onshape export,
  1.218). The link tree, mesh references, origins and inertials below are the
  export verbatim — the CAD geometry is already correct and matches config.h:

      joint1 -> joint2 axis distance = 0.2000 m = L2
      joint2 -> tool  axis distance = 0.2000 m = L3
      joint1 axis height            = 0.0843 m = L1

  Only the four movable joints were reworked, because the export does not agree
  with the firmware's IK frame (ArmKinematics.cpp) on names, signs or zero:

    1. RENAMED to joint1/joint2/joint3 so /joint_states from the bridge lines up
       with what the firmware means by q1/q2/q3.
    2. joint1 origin gains a 3.14159265358979 yaw. The export's zero pose puts the arm along
       -X; fk() puts it along +X. Absorbing the half turn into joint1's origin
       (rather than rotating base_link) keeps the world frame Z-up and X-forward.
    3. joint1 and joint2 axes are NEGATED. After the yaw above, the export's world
       axes come out (0,0,-1) and (0,+1,0); fk() needs (0,0,+1) and (0,-1,0) so
       that +q1 is CCW seen from above and +q2/+q3 RAISE the arm. joint3 already
       agrees and is untouched.
    4. joint1..3 became `revolute` and carry the config.h software travel limits.
    5. joint2_belt (the export's gm3506_revolute_1) mimics joint2 at the belt
       ratio, since the GM3506 pulley and the arm are one mechanism, not two DOF.

  Verified numerically against fk(): see test/test_kinematics.py.

  CONSTANTS MIRRORED FROM firmware/include/config.h. The firmware is the
  authority; this model only has to agree with it. Keep in sync by hand:

      limit lower/upper = -2.0 / 2.0 rad      J1_MIN..J3_MAX
      joint2_belt multiplier = 3.0            JOINT_GEAR[1]  (J2 belt is 3:1)

  effort=5.0 / velocity=10.0 are placeholders — not yet measured against the
  real actuators. They keep the URDF valid and are what MoveIt/ros2_control
  would read later; nothing in the twin uses them today.

  Plain URDF rather than xacro on purpose: the pixi env carries no xacro, and
  the geometry constants come from CAD so they cannot be parameterised anyway.
  Only the handful of values above would benefit — worth revisiting when MoveIt
  or ros2_control arrives and `ros-lyrical-xacro` gets added to pixi.toml.

  RE-EXPORTING FROM ONSHAPE: replace full_assembly.urdf, then re-apply the five
  changes above and re-run the test. Everything else can be copied straight over.
-->
<robot name="mini_ranka">

    <link name="base">
        <inertial>
            <mass value="0.176007" />
            <origin xyz="6.77725e-18 0.000809394 0.0197186" rpy="0 -0 0" />
            <inertia ixx="0.000202067" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="0.000207068" iyz="-6.31167e-07" izz="0.000384759" />
        </inertial>
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="1.03684e-19 -2.1684e-18 0.02" rpy="0 -0 0" />
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="package://full_assembly/meshes/Base.stl" scale="1 1 1" />
            </geometry>
            <material name="0.615686_0.811765_0.929412_0.000000_0.000000">
                <color rgba="0.615686 0.811765 0.929412 1" />
            </material>
        </visual>
    </link>
    <link name="bearring">
